# Orchestrate project rules

Read `SPEC.md` at the repo root before editing anything. It is the canonical,
REQ-tagged requirements document — shared across Claude Code (`CLAUDE.md`),
Codex (`AGENTS.md`), and Cursor (this file). Do not restate or reinvent
requirements; if something here conflicts with `SPEC.md`, `SPEC.md` wins.

## Phase-gated workflow

Follow the pipeline in `SPEC.md` §0/§2 in order:
P0 Data Load/Validate → P1 Safety Gate → P2 Multimodal Ingestion →
P3 Personalization/Retrieval → P4 Decision Fusion/Confidence →
P5 Output Generation/Validation.

Do not implement a later phase before the current phase's REQ items are done
and smoke-tested. Inspect the actual CSVs in `dataset/` before assuming
schema — don't rely on the README's prose description alone.

## Phase planning

When asked to plan, scaffold, or generate implementation prompts for a
phase, use `.agents/skills/orchestrate-phase-planner/SKILL.md`.

## REQ traceability

Every non-trivial function should be attributable to a REQ ID from `SPEC.md`.
Prefer naming/comments that make the REQ ID discoverable via search.

## Commit hygiene

No AI attribution in commits, PR descriptions, or code comments. Reference
REQ IDs in commit messages where relevant.

## Cross-tool continuity

This repo is also worked on with Claude Code and Codex depending on rate
limits. Leave the working tree resumable by another tool from `SPEC.md` +
repo state alone — don't leave a phase half-applied without a clear note.

## Secrets

Read API keys from environment variables only. Never hardcode a key or write
one into any file in this repo, including scratch scripts.

## Transcript logging

Do not touch or remove the `AGENTS.md` transcript-logging behavior — it's a
required submission artifact per `README.md`, independent of which tool is
currently active.